 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-363845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | ${x}_{1}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c55065d-263c-577d-a3d6-fb804f092192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423858 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{4}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7cbede22-0844-5470-8655-1901a08d01cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423859 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,2 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80997e16-c7cf-546e-901a-0d8f5c371714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423860 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}