center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423861 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d7c03f5-6a85-5299-a2e8-604190d0a4f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423861 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423862 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423862 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423862 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423862 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac399096-85d0-5a5b-9665-e0967589e76d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423862 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-9I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-363834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423863 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ b